# Two sites: the push wake-up gateway and its mediator. With SITE_SCHEME=https
# (the default) Caddy obtains and renews both certificates automatically; each
# host needs a public DNS
# A/AAAA record pointing here and ports 80 and 443 open (README
# "Prerequisites"). Environment placeholders are substituted before parsing.
#
# No ACME contact email is set; add a global `{ email ops@example.org }` block
# at the top of this file if you want expiry notices.

# POST /trust-tasks and GET /healthz. The gateway serves /metrics only on its
# own loopback listener, so there is nothing to route; the explicit 404 keeps it
# that way if that ever changes upstream.
{$SITE_SCHEME:https}://{$GATEWAY_HOST} {
	respond /metrics* 404
	reverse_proxy gateway:8300
}

# The mediator the gateway receives wake-ups through. Agents' mediators relay to
# it on /mediator/v1/inbound; its DID document names this site's https:// and
# wss:// URLs, and reverse_proxy carries the websocket upgrade.
#
# Its login endpoint (/mediator/v1/authenticate…) answers only private
# addresses. Inside this stack the mediator's hostname resolves to Caddy (the
# network alias in docker-compose.yml), so the gateway logs in from a private
# address; from the internet the endpoint is refused, so no stranger can hold
# a session: all they can do is hand the mediator a relayed message.
# (Unverified: whether it relays a stranger's message onward to a third party.
# The lab proof checks it; see HANDOVER.md §14.)
{$SITE_SCHEME:https}://{$MEDIATOR_HOST} {
	@public_login {
		path /mediator/v1/authenticate*
		not remote_ip private_ranges
	}
	respond @public_login 403
	reverse_proxy mediator:7037
}
